course: dictionary
lesson: 5152
title: Irony: the twist
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Ironic means strange or funny because something is the opposite of what you would expect. «It's ironic that the fire station burned down» is a twist, because fire stations are there to put fires out.
explain: Ironic can also mean saying the opposite of what you mean, as a joke. If it is pouring with rain and someone says «Great weather!», they are being ironic. You can tell from their tone and the situation that they mean the weather is awful.
explain: Writers use irony to surprise the reader. Ask yourself: what did I expect to happen, and what actually happened? The gap between those two is where the irony lives.
fact: Ironic means strange or funny because it is the opposite of what you would expect.
fact: Ironic can also mean saying the opposite of what you mean, as a joke.
fact: «It's ironic that the fire station burned down» is a twist, because fire stations are there to put fires out.
fact: In stories, dramatic irony means the reader knows something the characters do not.
fact: A statue of Hume on the Royal Mile in Edinburgh has a shiny toe from people rubbing it for luck, which is ironic because Hume doubted such beliefs.
exercise: «It's ironic that the fire station burned down.» What makes this ironic? | The fire was very hot | *The station's job is to put out fires, so burning down is the opposite of what you would expect | It happened on a Monday | Fire stations are very tall
exercise: In the rain, Sam says, «Great weather!» What is Sam doing? | Telling the truth about the sunshine | *Saying the opposite of what he means, as a joke | Singing about the rain | Asking for an umbrella
exercise: People rub Hume's statue's toe for luck, but Hume doubted luck. Why is this ironic? | *The people believe in something Hume did not believe in | The toe is cold | Hume was a famous runner | The statue is made of bronze
test: «The lifeguard could not swim.» Why is this ironic? | Lifeguards always swim in the sea | The lifeguard was wearing a hat | Swimming is a sport in the Olympics | *A lifeguard is meant to be a strong swimmer, so this is the opposite of what you would expect
test: Ella says, «Lovely day for a picnic!» while hail bounces off the table. What does ironic mean here? | She is asking for hail | She means the weather is perfect, and she is telling the truth | She is describing a picnic menu | *She means the weather is awful, and she is saying the opposite as a joke
exam: Which word means strange or funny because something is the opposite of what you would expect? | Curt | Barely | *Ironic | Famished
